WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.040
Welcome back to First Principles Daily, Episode 38.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:08.160
Starting the week by asking what things should actually cost, a routine dental filling

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.480
can cost hundreds while the materials inside are worth only a few dollars, showing where

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.020
the real price drivers sit.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:19.720
Consider a dental crown that reaches the patient at roughly $1,000.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:26.680
Inside that crown sits metal or ceramic stock worth perhaps $20 once the atoms are arranged.

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.100
The difference does not come from exotic elements or scarce minerals.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:36.560
Instead, it accumulates through the sequence of appointments, the specialized labor in

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.060
each chair, the small practice overhead, and the regulatory scaffolding that surrounds

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:42.800
every step.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.400
That gap is the opening the show we'll examine today.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:52.500
Dental work still follows a model built around the solo or small group practice that emerged

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.500
decades ago.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.500
A patient typically books an exam, returns for preparation, and returns again for seating.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.620
Each visit consumes chair time from a clinician whose training spans four years of dental school

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.660
plus residency.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.280
Most offices maintain their own imaging equipment, sterilization suites, and billing staff because

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.200
referral networks remain limited.

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:21.420
Insurance reimbursement schedules and state-by-state licensing rules further lock the pattern in place,

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.040
so the same procedure is repriced rather than redesigned.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:30.880
A composite filling, for example, is placed by hand under magnification with incremental

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.720
curing and polishing that can occupy 30 to 40 minutes of the dentist's time.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.200
Crowns follow a similar path.

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:43.500
An impression or scan leads to an off-site lab that fabricates the restoration over days

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.360
or weeks after which the patient returns for trial and adjustment.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:53.160
These steps feel normal inside the profession because each one was added incrementally to

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:57.380
solve yesterday's problems of fit, infection control, or liability.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:03.460
The cumulative effect is that the delivered price reflects accumulated labor hours, facility

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:08.420
costs, and administrative layers far more than the physical contents of the restoration

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:08.880
itself.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:14.440
Prevention programs exist but rarely scale to the point where they shrink the overall

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:18.240
volume of restorative work enough to change practice economics.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.180
As a result, the current price structure continues to appear inevitable to those working within

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:24.400
it.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.980
One objection often raised is that each added visit improves diagnostic certainty or reduces

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:31.820
error rates.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:38.100
Yet the data on multi-visit versus single-visit protocols show that the incremental benefit

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.840
plateaus once basic imaging and isolation standards are met.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:47.020
Another entrenched factor is the capital tied up in underutilized equipment.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:52.940
A practice that owns a cone beam scanner may spread its depreciation across fewer procedures

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:58.440
than a high-volume clinic would, raising the per case overhead even when the scan itself

00:02:58.440 --> 00:02:59.500
takes only seconds.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:05.540
Billing layers compound the total because each claim must navigate payer-specific coding,

00:03:06.080 --> 00:03:11.340
prior authorization steps, and denial appeals that can consume staff hours equal to the

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:12.500
clinical time itself.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:18.380
These frictions are rarely questioned inside the system because every participant optimized

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:23.460
for the constraints visible at the time, rather than for the physics of the tooth or the

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:25.940
commodity price of the restorative material.

00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:32.160
If a magic wand could assemble the finished restoration from raw atoms at commodity prices,

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:35.360
the floor would be set by the actual materials.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:41.740
A typical amalgam or resin composite filling uses on the order of a gram or two of alloy

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:46.820
or filled polymer whose commodity value sits in the low single-digit dollars.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:53.260
Even a full ceramic crown draws from perhaps ten to twenty dollars of zirconia or lithium

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:54.320
desilicate blank.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:04:00.840
Once the blank itself is priced at scale, adding the cost of local anesthetic, a few cotton

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:06.020
rolls, and a matrix band still keeps the rough total under twenty dollars in most cases.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.120
Against a delivered fee that commonly ranges from two hundred to five hundred dollars

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:16.580
for the filling and eight hundred to fifteen hundred for the crown, the implied idiot index

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.120
lands somewhere between ten and seventy-five.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:25.760
That ratio is only an estimate because exact material invoices vary by supplier and volume.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:31.720
Yet the order of magnitude is consistent with published dental supply catalogs and procedure

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:32.660
fee surveys.

00:04:33.160 --> 00:04:35.900
The gap does not sit inside the filling material.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.360
It sits in the sequence that surrounds it.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:44.340
Chairside time from a highly trained dentist accounts for the largest single slice, followed

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:50.100
by the fixed costs of the operator, the staff required for scheduling and claims, and

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.600
the carrying cost of the multi-visit workflow.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.540
Digital impression systems and in-office mills can compress some of those steps, yet

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:04.200
adoption remains uneven because capital outlay and training requirements favor larger practices.

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:10.140
Fragmented ownership also prevents the volume purchasing and standardized protocols that

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:12.880
would otherwise drive the per unit overhead down.

00:05:13.860 --> 00:05:18.580
Each of these layers adds to the finished price without changing the atoms inside the tooth.

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.440
To see the arithmetic more clearly, consider a zirconia crown.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:25.520
The blank may cost twelve dollars at wholesale.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:30.320
The milling burst set adds another three dollars amatricized across uses.

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:36.540
The centering furnace electricity perhaps one dollar per part, even generous allowances for

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:41.040
scrap and quality rejects keep the physical inputs below twenty-five dollars.

00:05:41.780 --> 00:05:46.960
The remaining nine hundred or more dollars therefore trace to the dentist's preparation appointment.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.300
The lab technicians design time or the in-office mill operator supervision.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.260
The second seating visit and the practices rent insurance and compliance overhead spread across

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:04.500
the case. One common objection is that material prices themselves fluctuate or that premium

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.880
blanks carry hidden quality premiums, yet even doubling the blank cost to forty dollars changes

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:16.860
the idiot index only from roughly forty to roughly twenty-five, still leaving the dominant

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:22.700
share of the price outside the atoms. Another objection holds that patient-specific anatomy

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:29.000
requires custom labor that cannot be standardized. The counter observation is that once a digital

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:35.160
scan exists, the geometric variability is captured in software parameters rather than in additional

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:41.220
physical labor hours, which is why centralized milling centers already demonstrate lower unit

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:47.740
costs when volume justifies the capital. A redesign would begin by shifting routine preventative

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:53.580
and simple restorative tasks to hygienists and therapists operating under clearer protocols,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:59.880
which several jurisdictions already allow at pilot scale. That single move reduces the

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:05.080
minutes a dentist must spend per patient and thereby lowers the dominant labor cost.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.360
Prevention that actually reaches daily brushing, flossing, and fluoride regimens at

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:15.820
population level would shrink total demand. The arithmetic is straightforward because

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:20.780
each avoided filling removes both the material and the entire service chain around it.

00:07:21.420 --> 00:07:27.460
Standardized intraoral imaging combined with teladentistry review could triage cases so that

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:32.540
only those needing physical intervention reach a chair, cutting unnecessary visits.

00:07:33.340 --> 00:07:39.240
Digital fabrication offers a third lever. Once a crown or a liner is designed from a scan,

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:45.260
additive or subtractive manufacturing at centralized facilities can produce the part overnight

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:51.480
at a fraction of traditional lab cost, provided regulatory pathways treat the file transfer as

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:57.300
equivalent to a physical impression. Each of these changes requires concrete conditions,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:04.380
expanded scope of practice statutes, interoperable scan formats, and liability frameworks that assign

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:10.660
responsibility clearly before the cost can move toward the material floor. Safety and outcome data

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:16.680
must remain the gate. No redesign can bypass controlled trials that demonstrate equivalent or better

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:22.120
longevity and lower complication rates. The hard limits are therefore not the physics of the

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:28.120
materials but the licensing, training, and evidence thresholds that correctly protect patients while

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:34.460
still allowing process innovation. A further objection is that shifting tasks risks missing subtle

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:40.500
pathology. The practical response is staged oversight where the first several hundred cases under

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:46.540
new protocols are double reviewed after which statistical monitoring replaces constant direct

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:52.500
supervision exactly as has occurred in other fields when diagnostic algorithms matured.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:59.260
Another constraint is capital concentration. Centralized mills require predictable case volume,

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:05.700
which fragmented practices cannot guarantee so the first adopters are likely to be dental service

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:12.020
organizations already operating dozens of locations. Policy changes that permit cross-state

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:18.120
teledentistry review would further enlarge the addressable volume and thereby justify the capital

00:09:18.120 --> 00:09:24.820
outlay sooner. One principle is that when clinician minutes dominate the bill any safe transfer of

00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:30.600
tasks to less specialized operators immediately changes the arithmetic of the entire service.

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:36.780
A second principle is that prevention and digital standardization attack demand and fabrication

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:43.380
cost at once rather than merely trimming the price of an unchanged workflow. The open question is

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:49.080
which dental service organization or regulatory body will publish the first controlled data set

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:54.700
showing both lower total cost and maintained clinical outcomes after these shifts are implemented

00:09:54.700 --> 00:10:01.860
at scale. That's first principle's daily for today. If this changed how you see what things cost share

